---
import { specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Vision & Roadmap"
  description="The long-term research direction for RoboLang and the planned stages after the read-only developer tools. Nothing on this page beyond language 1.1 is available yet."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Vision &amp; roadmap</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Where RoboLang is trying to go, and how that differs from what you can install today.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>This page describes plans, not features.</strong> Only the first stage below exists.
        Nothing here about execution, simulation, safety enforcement, interoperability, multi-robot
        communication or AI is available in the supported package, and plans may change.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Vision</h2>
    <p>
      The research question behind RoboLang is whether one small, precisely specified task language
      can sit between the people and AI systems that decide what a robot should do and the varied
      software that makes robots move.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Interoperable tasks.</strong> The same task description, checked once, understood by
        different robots through adapters that declare what each robot can do.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-robot communication.</strong> Structured, versioned messages for handing work
        from one robot to another, in place of free text.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI-generated programs.</strong> A language strict enough that a task written by a
        language model can be rejected with a precise reason before it reaches anything physical.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The project builds toward this in order: a correct language first, then execution in simulation,
      then interoperability. Each stage is meant to be verifiable before the next begins.
    </p>

    <h2>Stages</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Stage</th>
            <th scope="col">Focus</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">v1.1</th>
            <td>
              Language correctness: specification and grammar, parser, static validation, versioned IR,
              the read-only <code>validate</code> and <code>inspect</code> commands and an installable
              package.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Available</strong> as distribution {versions.distribution}
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">v1.2</th>
            <td>
              Execution correctness: an executor with explicit states, evaluated preconditions, a
              deterministic simulator and audit trail, and a robust ROS 2 adapter.
            </td>
            <td>Planned. Not started.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">v1.3</th>
            <td>
              Interoperability: robot capability descriptors, more than one simulated robot running the
              same task, and a structured communication and handoff protocol.
            </td>
            <td>Planned. Not started.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">v2.0</th>
            <td>AI-native RoboLang: generation and checking of tasks with language models.</td>
            <td>Planned. Not started.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The stage numbers are language and project milestones. They are separate from the distribution
      version of the Python package; see
      <a href="/get-started/#versions">the three version numbers</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full plan, with the scope and exit criteria proposed for each stage, is the
      <a href={specs.developmentPlan}>development plan</a> in the core repository.
    </p>

    <h2>Physical robots</h2>
    <p>
      Running RoboLang against real hardware is outside the current scope of the project. The supported
      package cannot do it, and the older prototype code in the repository has never been validated
      against ROS 2, a simulator or a robot. Please do not connect RoboLang to physical equipment.
    </p>

    <h2>Help shape it</h2>
    <p>
      The most useful input at this stage is on the language itself: what is awkward to express, which
      diagnostics are unclear and what a consumer of the JSON IR needs. See
      <a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
